d `_default_handler` reuse; new formatter applied on each `_setup_logger()` call.\n\nBut how is re-init triggered? _setup_logger() called at module import; \"重新初始化日志\" — tests may call logger._setup_logger() directly. Fine.\n\nAlso max_message_chars validation: int >= 32, not bool.\n\nFormat method: compute record.message after super().format? We need to truncate the message before formatting prefix, but multiline prefix handling uses record.message. Careful: don't modify record.msg/args. Approach: build message from record.getMessage(), truncate, then use a copy of record for formatting? Simplest: use logging.Formatter.format with a shallow copy of record with modified msg/args.\n\nStandard approach: \n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        msg = record.getMessage()\n        if len(msg) > self.max_message_chars:\n            record = copy.copy(record)  # shallow copy\n            record.msg = self._truncate(msg)\n            record.args = None\n    ...\n```\nNeed copy module. Copy of LogRecord: shallow copy shares exc_info etc but we don't modify them; setting msg/args on copy leaves original intact. But copy.copy of LogRecord: LogRecord has __dict__; copy works. Also `record.message` needs reset — the copy may have 'message' attribute from earlier formatting by another handler. Safer: delete copied record.message if present: `record.__dict__.pop(\"message\", None)`. Actually logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage(). If record already has message attr, it gets overwritten anyway. Fine.\n\nAlso for truncation on multiline: format() then the prefix logic uses record.message. Our truncated message goes to record.message after super().format. Good.\n\nWait — exc_info text applies after; exception info appended to output but the record's exc_info unchanged. Good. stack_info is added via record.stack_info in Formatter.format: it appends stack_info — but the truncated message doesn't affect it.\n\nImplement:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be None or an int >= {_MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET}, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < _MIN_MESSAGE_BUDGET:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n```\n\nTruncation helper:\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(msg: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    if len(msg) <= budget:\n        return msg\n    remaining = budget - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return msg[:head] + _OMITTED_MARKER + (msg[-tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\")\n```\n\nNote remaining could be negative if budget < 15, but budget>=32 so fine.\n\n_format: in format():\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n                record.args = None\n                record.__dict__.pop(\"message\", None)\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\nNote `record.__dict__.pop(\"message\", None)` — LogRecord stores message in __dict__? `record.message = ...` sets an attribute → in __dict__. Yes.\n\nCareful: `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True` — if attribute is 1, `1 is not True` → True → applies truncation. Good.\n\n_setup_logger: add max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS.\n\nNow stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nAlso the environ.py — let me verify its current content is correct (int_or_none_min exists, error message includes var name via descriptor wrapping). Wait: error message must contain the variable name. The descriptor wraps: `raise type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. For ValueError that works. Good. It's already applied.\n\nLet me now apply edits to logger.py and stats_logger.py. I need file hashes: logger.py 5b8f..., stats 9ef9....\n\nAlso need `import copy` in logger.py.\n\nNow also consider: envs may not be accessible as `from deepinfer import envs` in logger.py due to import order — deepinfer/__init__.py may import logger. Let's check deepinfer/__init__.py.\n\nLet me batch: read deepinfer/__init__.py and apply the logger edits? Better do read first quickly along with edits. I'll do the edits with `from deepinfer import envs` inside _setup_logger (deferred import) to avoid circularity. Actually cleaner: import at module top if safe. Use deferred import inside _setup_logger to be safe? Tests might check integration; deferred import is fine functionally. But hmm, importing deepinfer.environs at top of logger: environ.py doesn't import logger. And deepinfer/__init__.py — if it imports logger, then `from deepinfer import envs` at logger import time could trigger partial init. Use `from deepinfer.environ import envs` — that imports deepinfer package first anyway (parent package init runs). Deferring inside function is safest.\n\nI'll do deferred import in _setup_logger.\n\nLet me write the edits."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8840,"completion_tokens":1778,"total_tokens":10618,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1641},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":4096,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":4744},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}